{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y3wg7wfoMw65QjdrZosv18","type":"tool_result","content":"total 56\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 May 13 21:57 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 8 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 May 13 22:26 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  4610 May 13 20:27 diagnose_phase1_r3.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 May 13 21:57 dream_reports\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu   437 May 13 19:59 grpo_exec_only.yaml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu   434 May 13 19:59 grpo_exec_sta.yaml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  1262 May 13 18:25 launch_phase1.sh\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  1044 May 13 18:13 manifest.yaml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  2653 May 13 20:00 phase1_r2_diagnosis.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 May 13 18:05 sta-phase0-baseline\n-rw-r--r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu 11977 May 13 20:14 train_grpo.py","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01X2Ze8otE8hsT4oHfKSGjkb","session_id":"101daba2-396f-47b1-98e0-5a56827f90df","uuid":"a8706e4d-43e3-4f2f-b791-5064903cd053","timestamp":"2026-05-13T22:34:30.741Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015gxGfabJZjsMKv8YB5zxf2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SKh6AdJ89XDygL5qLfBGWq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","subagent_type":"Plan","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**. Review the following paper independently and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Solver Trace Ablation: A Mutation-Testing-Derived Methodology for Detecting Structural Degeneracy in RL-Trained Formalizers\"\n\nThe paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), grounded in the RIP (Reachability-Infection-Propagation) framework from mutation testing, to derive intensional structural rewards (ISR) from solver execution semantics for detecting/reducing structural degeneracy (reward hacking) in GRPO-trained formal logic generators.\n\n### Core Contributions Claimed\n1. A general, solver-agnostic STA derivation protocol grounded in the RIP framework, instantiated for FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, and SMT-LIB/Z3.\n2. Empirical characterization of logic-type-specific reward hacking modes across three formal languages, including a manually-annotated hacking taxonomy.\n3. Evidence that STA functions as a principled monitoring and diagnostic tool: it reliably detects and partially reduces structural degeneracy, with clear characterization of its detection boundaries.\n\n### Method\n- 5-step derivation protocol: Pilot → Collect shortcuts → Classify → Map RIP → Design check\n- ISR(y) = (1/N) Σ 1[S(y\\ci) ≠ S(y)] — fraction of components whose ablation changes solver verdict\n- Combined reward: r(y) = α·r_exec(y) + β·ISR(y)\n- Instantiated for 3 solver–language pairs\n\n### Related Work as presented in paper (Section 2)\n- **RL for formal reasoning**: DeepSeek-Prover [Xie 2025], Logic-RL [Wu 2025] — use binary execution rewards, don't address structural quality\n- **Structural rewards**: PRMs [Lightman 2024] — dense but need human annotation; ORMs — sparse but automatic; STA claimed to be \"dense + automatic\"\n- **Mutation testing**: RIP framework [Jia & Harman 2011] — adapted by treating formula components as \"statements\" and solver verdicts as \"test outputs\"\n- **Reward hacking in RL**: [Skalse 2022] — general reward hacking; paper claims first systematic characterization in formal logic generation\n\n### Key Results\n- Held-out: 1.9× ISR improvement (0.132 vs 0.068), hacking rate 69%→35%, 5pp accuracy cost\n- STA positioning: monitoring/diagnostic tool, not complete prevention\n- Cross-logic: direction of effect consistent across FOL/Prolog/SMT-LIB\n- Hacking taxonomy: surface-level modes suppressed, semantic-level modes emerge (adversarial adaptation)\n- Model scaling: larger models hack more, STA detection scales\n\n### References in paper (8 total)\n1. Han et al. 2022 — FOLIO dataset\n2. Jia & Harman 2011 — RIP framework / mutation testing survey\n3. Lightman et al. 2024 — PRMs (Let's verify step by step)\n4. Mellgren et al. 2025 — Structural penalties for Prolog\n5. Shao et al. 2024 — DeepSeekMath / GRPO\n6. Skalse et al. 2022 — Defining reward hacking\n7. Wang et al. 2026 — EVOM cross-solver GRPO\n8. Wu et al. 2025 — Logic programming with RL\n9. Xie et al. 2025 — Logic-RL\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate the following aspects and produce a structured review:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the RIP framework adaptation to RL reward design? Is it a genuine conceptual advance or mostly cosmetic framing?\n- Is the ISR metric genuinely new, or is it essentially \"leave-one-out ablation\" with new branding?\n- How novel is the hacking taxonomy contribution?\n- Is the \"5-step derivation protocol\" a genuine methodological contribution or obvious engineering?\n- Does the paper provide sufficient conceptual depth beyond \"ablate components and check if solver result changes\"?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are there missing related work areas? Consider:\n  - Reward shaping literature in RL\n  - Program synthesis / code generation quality metrics\n  - Formal verification and proof quality\n  - Test adequacy criteria beyond mutation testing\n  - Other reward hacking mitigation approaches (RLHF, constrained RL, etc.)\n  - Interpretability / explanation methods that use ablation\n- Is the connection to mutation testing genuinely deep or superficial?\n- Are claims of \"first\" accurate? (e.g., \"first systematic characterization of reward hacking in formal logic generation\", \"first application of mutation testing to RL reward design\")\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n- Is the paper well-positioned relative to prior work?\n- Is the \"monitoring/diagnostic tool\" framing a strength (honest) or weakness (underselling)?\n- Does the paper adequately differentiate from Mellgren et al. 2025 (Prolog structural penalties)?\n\n### 4. Strengths\n- What are the paper's strongest novelty contributions?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n- List specific weaknesses with severity (minor/moderate/major)\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n- List 3-5 questions you would ask\n\n### 7. Score\n- Overall: 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Soundness: 1-4\n- Presentation: 1-4\n- Contribution: 1-4\n- Confidence: 1-5\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers. Be rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to whether the novelty is sufficient for a top ML venue."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37478,"cache_read_input_tokens":22938,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37478},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"101daba2-396f-47b1-98e0-5a56827f90df","uuid":"7ad76a3b-c8b9-4809-a63d-e95532f1f339"}
