{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JfEvbdXdSvrc58hsgrv8hS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015F6LDPcrcg6rkJnYvs5DAb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T215750/worst/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5907,"cache_read_input_tokens":48266,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5907,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKh6AdJ89XDygL5qLfBGWq","session_id":"101daba2-396f-47b1-98e0-5a56827f90df","uuid":"508b490b-50b9-411c-a544-d9faffe6c613"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015F6LDPcrcg6rkJnYvs5DAb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{url}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formalizers:\\\\A Principled Negative Result}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tReinforcement learning with solver-verified rewards (e.g., GRPO with execution feedback) trains natural-language-to-formal-logic translators that achieve high execution accuracy but produce structurally degenerate outputs---a form of reward hacking.\n24\tWe introduce \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, a diagnostic and reward framework grounded in the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) model from mutation testing.\n25\tSTA derives an \\textbf{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)} by systematically ablating proof components and measuring their contribution to solver success.\n26\tWe report a principled negative result: while STA as a \\emph{post-hoc diagnostic} effectively detects and categorizes reward hacking (ISR sensitivity $\\approx$70\\% across four identified hacking modes), STA as a \\emph{training reward} actively harms performance.\n27\tAdding ISR to GRPO reduces prove rate from 0.24 to 0.11 without improving structural quality (ISR/PR ratio: 0.29 vs.\\ 0.27).\n28\tWe trace this failure to gradient variance amplification in a low-prove-rate regime where ISR signal is too sparse for stable policy optimization.\n29\tA 200-sample annotation study identifies four distinct hacking modes---tautology injection (28\\%), vacuous quantification (19\\%), premise redundancy (14\\%), and trivial conclusion (8\\%)---providing the first fine-grained taxonomy of structural degeneracy in GRPO-trained formalizers.\n30\tWe further show that supervised finetuning (SFT) on gold translations achieves both higher accuracy (0.30 vs.\\ 0.24) and substantially better structural quality (ISR/PR: 0.50 vs.\\ 0.29) than GRPO, suggesting that GRPO's on-policy exploration inherently amplifies solver-exploitable shortcuts.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t\\section{Introduction}\n34\t\n35\tRecent work has demonstrated that Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with solver-verified execution rewards can train language models to translate natural language into formal logic with increasing accuracy \\cite{shao2024deepseek}.\n36\tHowever, execution accuracy alone is a deceptive metric: a model can achieve high prove rates by generating structurally degenerate formalizations that exploit solver behavior rather than faithfully representing the source semantics.\n37\t\n38\tThis reward hacking phenomenon is well-documented in broader RL \\cite{skalse2022defining} but poorly characterized in the neurosymbolic formalization setting.\n39\tExisting structural reward designs are ad-hoc: Mellgren et al.\\ hand-craft Prolog-specific structure penalties, while execution-only approaches like EVOM use scalar solver feedback without structural analysis.\n40\tNo prior work provides a \\emph{principled derivation procedure} for structural rewards from solver semantics.\n41\t\n42\tWe address this gap by introducing \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, adapting the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) framework from mutation testing to RL reward design.\n43\tThe key idea is simple: a component of a formal proof is \\emph{structurally necessary} if and only if its removal changes the solver's verdict.\n44\tBy systematically ablating each component and re-running the solver, STA produces a continuous \\textbf{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)} that quantifies the degree to which each proved output relies on genuinely necessary components versus structural shortcuts.\n45\t\n46\tWe report a principled negative result with three contributions:\n47\t\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{STA as diagnostic framework.} We present a 5-step derivation protocol for constructing ISR from any solver--language pair and demonstrate that ISR effectively detects structural degeneracy. On held-out evaluation, ISR identifies that 74\\% of exec-only GRPO outputs are structurally degenerate---information invisible to execution accuracy alone.\n50\t\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{STA as training reward fails.} Adding ISR to GRPO's reward function reduces prove rate from 0.24 to 0.11 across all tested weight configurations ($\\alpha/\\beta$ ratios and schedules) without improving structural quality. We provide a mechanistic explanation: in the low-prove-rate regime ($<$20\\%), ISR's effective per-step sample size ($K \\times \\text{prove\\_rate} \\approx 1.6$) is insufficient for stable policy gradient estimation.\n52\t\n53\t    \\item \\textbf{Hacking taxonomy.} A 200-sample annotation study ($\\kappa = 0.72$) identifies four distinct reward hacking modes with differential ISR sensitivity, providing the first fine-grained characterization of structural degeneracy in GRPO-trained formalizers.\n54\t\\end{enumerate}\n55\t\n56\t\\section{Related Work}\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{RL for formal reasoning.}\n59\tGRPO \\cite{shao2024deepseek} uses group-relative advantages for policy optimization without a critic model.\n60\tRecent work applies GRPO to mathematical reasoning and code generation with execution-verified rewards.\n61\tEVOM demonstrates cross-solver GRPO transfer using scalar execution rewards.\n62\tOur work extends this line by investigating \\emph{structural} reward signals derived from solver traces.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Reward hacking in RL.}\n65\tReward hacking occurs when an agent exploits discrepancies between the reward function and the intended objective \\cite{skalse2022defining}.\n66\tIn language model alignment, this manifests as reward model overoptimization \\cite{gao2023scaling}.\n67\tWe identify an analogous phenomenon in neurosymbolic formalization: models exploit solver behavior to achieve high execution accuracy without faithful semantic translation.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Mutation testing and the RIP framework.}\n70\tMutation testing assesses test suite adequacy by introducing small syntactic changes (mutants) and checking whether tests detect them \\cite{jia2011analysis}.\n71\tThe RIP framework formalizes mutant detection as requiring Reachability (the mutated statement executes), Infection (the mutation alters program state), and Propagation (the altered state affects the output).\n72\tSTA adapts this framework: instead of mutating a program to test a test suite, we ablate proof components to test structural necessity.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Structural rewards for formalization.}\n75\tExecVerify and EGCA derive trace-level rewards for code generation.\n76\tMellgren et al.\\ design Prolog-specific structure penalties.\n77\tOur STA framework differs in providing a \\emph{general derivation procedure} applicable to any solver--language pair, grounded in the formal RIP framework rather than ad-hoc heuristics.\n78\t\n79\t\\section{Method}\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n82\t\n83\tGiven a natural language statement $s$, a formalizer $\\pi_\\theta$ generates a formal translation $f \\sim \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid s)$.\n84\tA solver $\\mathcal{S}$ evaluates $f$, returning a binary verdict $\\mathcal{S}(f) \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ (proved/not proved).\n85\tStandard GRPO uses $r_{\\text{exec}}(f) = \\mathcal{S}(f)$ as the reward signal.\n86\t\n87\tThe reward hacking problem arises because $\\mathcal{S}(f) = 1$ does not imply that $f$ faithfully represents $s$.\n88\tA degenerate formalization $f^*$ may be provable via structural shortcuts (e.g., tautology injection) without capturing the semantics of $s$.\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}\n91\t\n92\tSTA derives a structural reward by measuring the necessity of each component in a proved formalization.\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{5-step derivation protocol.}\n95\tGiven a solver $\\mathcal{S}$ and formal language $\\mathcal{L}$:\n96\t\\begin{enumerate}\n97\t    \\item \\textbf{Pilot study}: Run $\\mathcal{S}$ on a sample of model outputs to assess prove rate and identify common output structures. Proceed only if prove rate $>$ 5\\% (go/no-go gate).\n98\t    \\item \\textbf{Shortcut collection}: Manually inspect proved outputs to catalog structural shortcuts (e.g., tautological premises, vacuous quantifiers).\n99\t    \\item \\textbf{RIP classification}: For each shortcut type, determine which RIP condition it violates---Reachability (component never used by solver), Infection (component removal doesn't change solver state), or Propagation (state change doesn't affect verdict).\n100\t    \\item \\textbf{Ablation mapping}: Design component-level ablation operations that test for RIP violations. For FOL+Prover9: remove each premise/axiom and re-run the prover."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKh6AdJ89XDygL5qLfBGWq","session_id":"101daba2-396f-47b1-98e0-5a56827f90df","uuid":"39e8e326-9bae-4a38-9799-6fa5364b9d9a","timestamp":"2026-05-13T22:35:50.247Z"}
